window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"20-30","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"20-30-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-01/","title_he":"א – התעוררות הכפירה בדורות האחרונים","title_en":"The Awakening of Heresy in Recent Generations","body_en":"<p>In recent generations, through the development of the natural sciences and the sciences of man, society and culture, human consciousness has developed and expanded greatly.</p>\n<p>Many natural phenomena that man did not understand were investigated and became understood and explained. In the process, the value of some of the explanations of faith that were accepted in earlier generations was nullified. In the past, when the heavens were shut and no rain fell, the wheat did not grow and the water reservoirs emptied, and people died of hunger and thirst. When a plague spread, many people fell dead, with almost no way of defending themselves against the death that lay in wait for them. Even in the ordinary course of life man was exposed to grave dangers, such as a snakebite or the development of infectious diseases. Out of his distress man turned to God in prayer that He help him, and religious leaders encouraged people to pray and to keep the mitzvot, so that God would save them.</p>\n<p>By contrast, in recent generations methods were developed for storing water and conveying it through pipes, so that even in a severe drought people do not die of hunger and thirst. Thanks to scientific research, vaccines and medicines were found for diseases that in the past were incurable, and dangerous plagues almost never break out. Thus life expectancy has risen greatly. With the help of the social sciences, effective methods were developed for organising society and the economy, and methods were developed for providing relief for mental illnesses. With the help of the development of technology, the production of food, furniture, clothing and houses improved greatly, until today an average person can live a long life, and in more comfortable conditions than the great kings of the past. In such a situation, many people who had grown accustomed to praying and keeping mitzvot in order to be saved from famine and plague, from poverty and from servitude, felt less need to pray and to keep mitzvot. Their feeling was that science would continue to develop until it solved all of man's troubles, and no place would remain for religion in the world. On the other hand, men of spirit and religion rightly argued that it is impossible for science to solve all of man's troubles, since man aspires to a life full of moral and spiritual meaning without limit, whereas science can provide only comfortable conditions of existence and no more. Therefore the pretension would lead to bitter disappointment and to severe crises.</p>\n<p>However, no less than criticising the exaggerated pretension, it would have been fitting to rejoice in the development of science, and to bless God and thank Him for it, since it is God who created man in the image of God, and graced him with the understanding by means of which he can progress in understanding his world and improving his life. And so the Sages instituted a blessing on seeing one of the sages of the nations of the world: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who has given of His wisdom to flesh and blood\" (Peninei Halakha, Berakhot 15:18).</p>\n<p>But many religious leaders continued to base their arguments on belittling man, and warned that it is the evil inclination that entices man to grow proud and think that by his own strength and the might of his hand he can understand nature and improve the conditions of his life. Thus, instead of appreciating the fruits of science and blessing God for them, they held that one should keep one's distance from science and cast doubt on its reliability. At the same time, many people who appreciated science felt that religion was of no benefit to their lives and to the world.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-30-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-02/","title_he":"ב – התנגשות בין המדע לדת","title_en":"The Clash between Science and Religion","body_en":"<p>In the course of scientific discoveries, conceptions were rejected that had been accepted by religious leaders as by the rest of mankind. For example, it was accepted that the sun revolves around the earth, and it became clear to scientists that the earth revolves around the sun. It was accepted that there are no tiny creatures in the world that cannot be seen, and it became clear through observation in a microscope that they exist. On the other side, various religious leaders saw in the scientists' challenge to the old conceptions an affront to the honour of religion, for it was impossible that the scientists knew more than the eminent sages of religion who had lived in the past and accepted the science that was accepted in their day. Not only that, but at times the Sages of Israel made use of analogies from the science accepted in their day in order to explain spiritual ideas, and when the scientists refuted the old scientific positions, there were those who saw in this an affront to their honour and to their words of Torah, for they thought that the analogies too were Torah.</p>\n<p>Moreover, it was accepted among the Sages of Israel that the world was created less than six thousand years ago, whereas scientists in the fields of geology and physics claimed that the earth has existed for billions of years. In addition, various theories were developed concerning a gradual, evolutionary development of man from lower animals such as the ape, whereas in the Torah there is no trace of this. Thus a feeling arose as though science stood in contradiction to the Torah.</p>\n<p>One can of course explain that there is no contradiction between the Torah and the scientific theories, since the Torah explains the idea of creation and not the process of creation in its details. Therefore the six days can be interpreted as six eras lasting billions of years. One can also explain that the process of creation took place gradually, as explained in the theory of evolution (Orot Ha-kodesh 2, p. 537). The Sages of Israel even said that 'Ma'aseh Bereshit' (the work of creation) is a secret, and accordingly one need not cling to its plain meaning. Likewise one can explain that the Torah's intention is to describe the order of the revelation of faith to man, and therefore it begins with the story of Adam, the first man, into whom God breathed a divine soul, and he was the first who could hear the voice of God and choose whether to fulfil His command. And this does not rule out the possibility that perhaps before him there existed earlier creatures from which the first man developed.</p>\n<p>However, many religious sages, of Israel and of the nations of the world, saw in the challenge to the science that had been accepted in the past an affront to the great sages of religion. And when the position of the scientists contradicted descriptions apparently written in the Torah concerning the creation of the world, it was perceived as outright heresy. Thus there developed among some of the rabbis the position that science is dangerous to religion, and that one must fight it, or at the very least weaken its standing. To strengthen their position, various preachers began collecting cases in which scientists had erred, cases in which scientific developments had caused harm, and cases in which 'all the doctors' said that so-and-so would die, and he prayed and was healed.</p>\n<p>And although the great Sages of Israel studied the sciences and encouraged their students to study them (Shabbat 75a); and the Rambam regarded the natural sciences as part of the great wisdom called 'Ma'aseh Bereshit' (Rambam, Commentary on the Mishna, Ḥagiga 2:1); and the Gra said that to the extent that a person lacks knowledge of the other wisdoms, he will correspondingly lack a hundredfold in the wisdom of the Torah, for the Torah and wisdom are bound together (Peninei Halakha, Likkutim I 1:15) – despite this, out of fear that the study of science would lead to heresy, there were rabbis who fought against it. To strengthen their position, preachers arose who claimed that there is no need to study the sciences, since all of them can be learned from the Torah. But with the help of science, ways continued to be broken open for increasing man's ability to create machines, to accelerate agriculture, to develop industry, commerce, means of transport and communication, and to accumulate wealth beyond measure. And the more the representatives of religion intensified their fight against science, the more the criticism of religion grew.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-30-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-03/","title_he":"ג – תנועת הנאורות וההשכלה","title_en":"The Enlightenment and Haskala Movement","body_en":"<p>In parallel with the scientific developments, the movement of Enlightenment and Haskala took shape in Western Europe, which strove for renewal (modernity) out of a belief that man, by the power of his thought and initiative, can change the world for the better and solve all the problems that stand before him. By its force great ideas developed for the repair of society, such as: liberalism, democracy, socialism, communism, national movements, and even fascism and Nazism. These ideas brought about far-reaching changes in the order of society, from monarchic rule that does not encourage free initiative to a government that grants man rights to express his talents in every field, or to a dictatorial regime intended 'to repair the world'. These movements greatly accelerated the development of science and the economy, and in turn intensified the social ideas and the need to implement them.</p>\n<p>The success of the Haskala movement in intensifying initiative, creativity, research and development among the peoples of Europe and North America created enormous wealth, which was invested in accelerating the development of science and the economy and in producing sophisticated weapons. With their help they took control of countries and continents throughout the world, exploited the natural resources and the people who lived in them, and bequeathed their culture to them, each stream according to its own way.</p>\n<p>Many of the first thinkers and researchers of the Enlightenment and Haskala movement, such as Descartes, Leibniz and Newton, believed in God. But the accepted religious conception, which emphasised the smallness of man, opposed the Haskala movement, and as a counter-reaction caused many of the supporters of the Haskala movement to distance themselves from religion. Later the position took shape within the Haskala movement that religion is harmful and holds back the development of humanity, and that instead of obeying the laws of religion, one should invest effort in social changes, in scientific research and in technological development, in order to redeem man from poverty and deprivation and to enable him to express the fullness of his powers.</p>\n<p>On the other side, many religious people saw in the pretension of the Haskala and Enlightenment movement to change the world a defiance of Heaven, since in their opinion man's role is to obey God and to pray to Him that He redeem man and the world (see above 18:12-16). Many warned that the audacity to challenge the order of the world would cause disasters. Criticism of the religious institutions would lead to denial of God and to a dangerous eruption of evil inclinations; rebellion against the monarchy would break society apart and cause bloodshed; intensifying the rights of the individual would break up the family and threaten social cohesion. Therefore man must place God at the centre (theocentrism), make himself small before God and the institutions that represent His governance, and not place himself at the centre (anthropocentrism).</p>\n<p>There was justice in the criticism of the religious people, for the Enlightenment movement gave birth to communism, fascism and Nazism, movements that championed denial of God and claimed 'to repair the world', but in practice caused terrible injustices and the murder of hundreds of millions of people. Even the capitalist-liberal stream, which did not inscribe denial of God on its banner and did not cause terrible disasters, caused many troubles. Among them: the subjugation of hundreds of millions of people to the control of the wealthy and of corporations; the subjugation of many peoples to the control of the Western peoples while harming their national and cultural identity; the weakening of the institution of the family; the erosion of man's confidence in his values and faith; and making man solitary in the face of life's challenges.</p>\n<p>But in the main debate about the place of man, the Haskala movement was right. For it is the Torah that taught that man was created in the image of God, and it is the Torah that placed man at the centre, in that all its mitzvot and guidance are directed to him, and it taught that God placed upon man the responsibility to guard the world, to lead it with justice and law and to perfect it until its redemption. Therefore the justified criticism of the religious people did not have the power to stop the Haskala movement. Thus, while striving for \"liberty, equality and fraternity\", the Haskala movement brought about far-reaching changes in the life of man and society. By its force kingdoms fell and in their place arose states that tried to realise the values of Haskala and Enlightenment, with the good and the bad in them. From the value of 'liberty' developed the liberal, democratic, capitalist and 'post-modern' stream, including its severe extremes. From the value of 'equality' developed the socialist stream, and from its extreme the communist stream. From the value of 'fraternity' developed the aspiration to establish nation states, and from its extreme developed the fascist and Nazi streams.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-30-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-04/","title_he":"ד – משבר החילון וההתבוללות","title_en":"The Crisis of Secularisation and Assimilation","body_en":"<p>For two reasons, the members of the Jewish people were especially drawn to the Haskala movement and gave it their energy and talent:</p>\n<p>The first reason: for many generations Jews lived in terrible humiliation and severe discrimination, and now a movement arose that claimed that all human beings were created equal, and strove to grant all of them, including the Jews, equal rights. With the success of the Haskala movement, the opportunity opened before the Jews to realise their talent and to integrate into the fields of the natural sciences and the humanities, the economy, society and the arts, and to attain wealth and honour. Jewish talent began to shine, and Jews began to lead in all the fields of science, society, the economy and the arts.</p>\n<p>The second reason: Jews always believed in the redemption of the world, and therefore the vision of the Haskala movement to redeem man from all his troubles resonated in Jewish hearts. The more the Haskala movement succeeded in actually bringing about revolutionary changes for the advancement of man and society, the more people began to believe that it was indeed a movement of redemption. Thus the messianic vision of the return of Israel to their land, in order to fulfil the word of God and bring blessing to all the families of the earth, was dimmed. In its place was set up a new messianic vision of social and moral reforms, and of the development of science and technology for the benefit of humanity.</p>\n<p>In contrast to what happened to all the other peoples who went through exile, throughout nearly two thousand years of exile, and despite all the suffering and persecution, the people of Israel succeeded in surviving, as God promised His people. This preservation was accomplished by a miracle that was realised through the ways of nature, that is, in a way that has a logical explanation. The explanation is that the vision of redemption of the people of Israel is so great and mighty that no exile and suffering could overcome it. As our master Rav Kook wrote: \"The anticipation of salvation is the sustaining force of exilic Judaism\" (Orot, Eretz Yisrael 1). But when an alternative messianic vision arose, the test became much harder.</p>\n<p>It is fitting to add that without the Jewish tradition and the prophetic messianic vision of redemption, the Haskala movement would not have arisen, because it would not have occurred to human beings to presume to bring about a revolution and change the world for the better. The Jewish heritage in the Tanakh concerning the Exodus from Egypt and the giving of the Torah, which brought about a revolutionary change in human consciousness, and the prophecies that continued to echo the vision of redemption, were the basis for the tremendous daring to create a movement striving for the repair of the world. Therefore many Jews felt that the Haskala movement, in its various branches, echoed what was hidden in their hearts, and when rabbis kept their distance from it, they argued against them: Here, when the redemption that we have all been awaiting is beginning to happen, you stand aloof and deny it. Thus many of Israel joined the Haskala movement, and in a gradual process distanced themselves from Judaism and assimilated among the gentiles.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-30-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-05/","title_he":"ה – הבחירה והאכזבה","title_en":"The Choice and the Disappointment","body_en":"<p>To explain what changed in recent generations, that suddenly the Haskala movement awoke and arose, one must contemplate God's governance of the world. Our master Rav Kook explained (Ma'amar Ha-dor) that as long as the decree of exile continued as a punishment for the sins of Israel, Jews bowed their heads and accepted their fate, for they felt that they deserved this, and that by this they were atoning for their sins and the sins of their fathers. Throughout that entire period, the preservation of Judaism was by means of a conservative stance that was wary of every innovation, since the main goal was to survive. This situation radiated onto all the nations, since Israel among the nations is like the heart among the organs.</p>\n<p>But punishment has a limit, and when the measure of suffering was full, the divine governance began to stir the hearts of human beings to recognise their greatness and reveal their talent. From this they began to seek liberty for man and for the nations, and to create economic, technological and transport conditions that make it possible to realise the vision of the redemption of Israel and the world through a miracle that appears in the way of nature. But as always, the choice is given to man, whether to express the greatness of man in pride and tyranny or in adding good. Before the Jewish people too, the heart of the nations, the choice stood: to join the worldwide Haskala movement and be absorbed into it, or to fulfil the divine command and go up to the Land, to grow great in Torah and mitzvot and establish a model society for the glory of our people and of humanity, until \"out of Zion shall go forth Torah, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem\" (Yeshayahu 2:3).</p>\n<p>Regrettably, most Jews gave up the unique vision of Israel and stopped keeping Torah and mitzvot. Later many of them suffered a bitter disappointment, when on the altar of the 'new world' they were forced to sacrifice everything dear to them, and even their lives. Many Jews who worked with self-sacrifice for the communist movement were executed in humiliation by the murderous Soviet regime, while understanding that the revolution in which they had been partners had caused terrible suffering to many people, and even to members of their own people. Many Jews who identified with German culture and contributed the best of their talent to it were humiliated and murdered. Through the fruits of the technological development of the Haskala movement, masses of Jews could have gone up to the Land and settled it, but instead of millions of Jews boarding the railway tracks on their way to the Land of Israel, on those tracks they were taken to the extermination camps. Also among the Jews who emigrated to the new world in America, and gave up the precious tradition and the vision of redemption in order to attain 'paradise on earth' – many were disillusioned and were forced to contend with antisemitism and discrimination, and a system of values that is far from ideal.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-30-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-06/","title_he":"ו – אופיו של הדור החדש","title_en":"The Character of the New Generation","body_en":"<p>As stated, despite all the justified criticisms of the Haskala movement, since its critics did not grasp the truth within it, they did not succeed in preventing the great torrent of masses of people who joined it and left religion. Our master Rav Kook explained (Ma'amar Ha-dor) that in all the generations until the modern era, those who abandoned the Torah were those who were drawn after their evil inclination, who stole and robbed, betrayed and committed adultery. But those who abandon the Torah in the new generation believe in the values of truth and goodness, and at times are even prepared to devote their lives to realising them. They believe that man can develop and understand more, be more moral, more creative and happier. But according to what they had been accustomed to hear, they perceived religion as something that deals in belittling man and making him obedient rather than an initiator, and that focuses on particular laws without setting forth a vision for the repair of the world. Therefore many of them abandoned religion and were drawn after ideas that captivated their hearts, without it ever occurring to them that it is precisely through the guidance of the Torah that all the new ideas could be intensified, refined of their dross, and realised in the most correct and best way.</p>\n<p>Therefore Rav Kook wrote that it is impossible to bring the new generation back in repentance by threats of punishment in this world or in the World to Come, and thereby to suppress the new ideas that stir its thought and heart. For \"even if it wishes to be bent and bowed\" it will not be able to bear it, for this is against its nature. \"It will not be able to return out of fear\", that is, threats of punishment, \"but it is very well suited to return out of love\" to the great vision. Therefore one must approach the members of the new generation with appreciation for their aspirations and desires. \"To them we must teach a Torah of life from the source of life, ways of ethics full of light and joy, words of grace and good sense, refined and purified… of the treasure of life for a Torah of life.\" \"We will not rob it of all the light and goodness, of all the radiance and strength that it has acquired for itself; rather, we will add to them, we will make shine upon them the light of life, the light of truth, that shines from the source of the Israelite soul. And our children will look to it and be radiant.\" \"We do not wish to crush beneath our feet; we are not willing to put in fetters the young and fresh forces that storm forth and rise up. Rather, we will illuminate the way before them, we will walk before them in a pillar of fire of Torah and holy knowledge, mighty in strength of heart\" (Eder Ha-yakar, p. 115).</p>\n<p>For this purpose the study of Torah in these generations must be with awareness of the general vision of the repair of the world in the Torah, and with a deep understanding that this great vision is revealed through all the general principles and details of the Torah (Orot Ha-teshuva 4:10). As our master Rav Kook explained, one must see in the guidance of the Torah how \"within all the details together is found the divine soul of the perfecting of the world, in life, in matter and spirit, in society and in the individual, and accordingly the light penetrates and descends as well into each and every detail\" (Orot Ha-Torah 2:2). In this way, not only will all the achievements of the natural sciences and the humanities not interfere with the revelation of faith or become a stumbling block for it, but they will intensify and elevate the revelation of faith, as will be explained in section 13.</p>\n<p>In order that Torah scholars be able to reveal the light within the Torah, they must stop fearing great and new ideas. As our master Rav Kook wrote (Ikvei Ha-tzon, 'Ha-paḥad'), in order to absorb the great light that is beginning to shine at the end of days, in the footsteps of the Messiah, one must remove the fear and weakness from which the righteous and pious, who are anxious to preserve the precious tradition, especially suffer. \"Then strength will be set in the setting of holiness, and thought will blossom.\"</p>\n<p>Although several generations have passed since our master Rav Kook wrote his words, the values of the Haskala movement are still the values that constitute the life of society in the developed countries, most of Israel still do not keep Torah and mitzvot properly, and many Jews, full of desire to make the world better, think that they will not find in the Torah a response to their aspiration.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-30-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-07/","title_he":"ז – המפתח לתיקון בזיכוך האמונה","title_en":"The Key to Repair Is Refining Faith","body_en":"<p>Our master Rav Kook explained (Orot, Zer'onim, 'Yissurim Memarkim') that all the perplexities and complications in man's life stem from a lack of understanding of the concept of Godliness, which is a sea without end. By nature man longs for nearness to God, and consequently wishes to define Him. But God is above and beyond every conception and definition. We can grasp only what is revealed to us, and therefore what can be said of God is that He is the one source of all ideas and values, powers and creatures. And anyone who tries to define Him Himself, or even the totality of the illumination that He bestows, sins with a trace of idolatry, limits the revelation of His light to certain realms, and consequently estranges himself from good ideas and values that also reveal His word.</p>\n<p>That is, when faith is defined narrowly, it does not contain all the ideas that are revealed in the world, and consequently there arises, as it were, a clash between faith and that idea. For example, when religious people think that God is revealed only through the scientific explanation accepted until now, a clash arises between faith and the new scientific positions. But in truth God is above all the scientific positions of all the generations, for in every scientific position a divine spark is revealed as befits that generation. And the more the scientific position develops, the more the word of God is revealed through it with added illumination. Likewise, when faith does not include all the sanctified values that find expression in practical life, a clash arises between them and faith. Similarly, when religious people think that the service of God is done only by way of submission, a clash arises between religion and the faith that God implanted in man, out of which he wishes to create and initiate with upright stature. But in truth God is revealed through all the powers together. Through creativity and initiative man draws the word of God and His blessing into the world, and through submission and caution he refines the evil dross that could cause his creativity and initiative to fail.</p>\n<p>Therefore, we must accustom ourselves to refine faith in such a way that through its study, faith will be in the One God, who is infinite perfection. That is, \"in the greatness of infinite perfection, such that whatever enters the heart is a spark entirely nullified in relation to what ought to be conceived, and what ought to be conceived does not even rise to the category of nullification in relation to what He truly is.\" Consequently it is clear that no faith in a finite and defined idea will express complete faith or set the mind at rest, and therefore there will forever be further renewals of movements that reveal faith without end.</p>\n<p>Out of this pure faith we will be able to see the light of God that is revealed in all the movements that are renewed and that will be renewed out of a quest for truth and goodness, for \"in everything there is a spark of light; the inner divine spark shines in each one of the various faiths.\" Therefore, instead of opposing them, we will increase the original light of Israel, in all its depth and breadth, and through this we will show how every good spark in every movement flows from its source, and then from all the sparks light and life will be added and renewed for all of Israel, and hearts thirsting for light will no longer stray in foreign fields, but will look and see that it is precisely through attachment to the faith of Israel that they will merit to realise all their aspirations (Orot, Zer'onim, 'Le-milḥemet Ha-de'ot Ve-ha-emunot', p. 131).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"Similarly our master Rav Kook wrote (Orot, Orot Ha-teḥiya 18): \"Three forces are now wrestling in our camp\": the religious faction, the national faction and the universal faction. Naturally the religious faction sees the other factions as rival movements. But people of faith are called upon to rise to a holy level from which they will be able to understand that in each of the factions a value of faith is revealed, and through attachment to the source of holiness all three will be able to fructify one another."}]},{"slug":"20-30-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-08/","title_he":"ח – מעמד הכופרים והטועים בעיקרי האמונה","title_en":"The Status of Heretics and Those Who Err in the Principles of Faith","body_en":"<p>Having examined the challenges to faith in recent generations, we turn to understand the attitude towards heretics in earlier generations and in our generation. As a rule, it is a mitzva to love every person of Israel, and even if he transgressed many sins out of his desire and the overpowering of his evil inclination, he is called a 'transgressor of Israel' and is punished for them, but he has not left the community of Israel, who have a share in the World to Come. And it is an obligation to love him and to have compassion on him, and to fulfil towards him all the mitzvot of brotherhood between the children of Israel. And one who excludes such sinners from the community of Israel is following 'the way of the heretics' ('Perek Be-hilkhot Tzibbur', Ma'amarei Ha-re'iya, p. 58).</p>\n<p>Moreover, the Rambam wrote (Iggeret Ha-shemad) that even one who converted from his religion under pressure, although he sinned a grave sin, remains within the community of Israel and should be drawn near and not pushed away, since he did not decide by free choice to deny the principles of the faith of Israel. However, sinners who have excluded themselves from the community of Israel, and determined for themselves that they are not part of the people of Israel, there is no obligation to love.</p>\n<p>In addition to those who exclude themselves from the community of Israel, according to the Rambam one who chose to deny one of the thirteen principles of faith also \"has left the community and denied the fundamental, and is called a 'min' (heretic), an 'apikoros' and 'one who cuts down the plantings', and it is an obligation to hate him and destroy him. And concerning him it says (Tehillim 139:21): Do I not hate those who hate You, O Lord\" (Commentary on the Mishna, Sanhedrin 10:1). However, even the Rambam concedes that those who deny one of the principles are considered Jews, and therefore they inherit their parents as Jews (Rambam, Hilkhot Naḥalot 6:12), they exempt the yevama (the widow of a childless brother) from levirate marriage through ḥalitza (Hilkhot Yibum Va-ḥalitza 1:4), and if they married according to the law of Moshe and Israel, their kiddushin are valid (Hilkhot Ishut 4:15). But since they denied one of the principles, they have no share in the World to Come, and in everything concerning the mitzvot of brotherhood one must relate to them as though they had left the community of Israel.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, many of the Sages of Israel held that one who errs in some of the principles of faith remains within the community of Israel with regard to the mitzvot of brotherhood and has a share in the World to Come. So wrote the Ra'avad, that many of Israel, among them also great sages, erred in thinking that one may attribute corporeal expressions to God, and one should not claim that because of this they left the community of Israel; rather, they have a share in the World to Come (his gloss on Hilkhot Teshuva 3:7). This is also the opinion of the Riaz (Kuntres Ha-re'ayot, Sanhedrin 90a). Similarly Rabbi Yosef Albo wrote (Sefer Ha-ikkarim 1:1) that it is impossible that one who denies the coming of the Messiah, which according to the Rambam is the twelfth principle, is considered as one who has left the community of Israel, for in the Talmud (Sanhedrin 99a) the opinion of the Amora Rabbi Hillel is cited, who said: \"There is no Messiah for Israel, for they already consumed him in the days of Ḥizkiyahu.\" Rav Yosef responded to his words: \"May his Master forgive Rabbi Hillel\", that is, may God forgive Rabbi Hillel, who erred in his words. And if Rabbi Hillel were considered a heretic who had left the community of Israel, why were his words cited in the Talmud? And if they cited his words in order to reject them, why, instead of defining him as a heretic who must be excommunicated and banned, did they honour him with the title 'Rabbi'? From here we see that even one who errs in grave matters is considered part of the community of Israel whom one must honour. And so wrote the Radbaz (4:187), that one who errs in one of the principles because of his mistaken inquiry is not considered a heretic but one acting under duress, since this is what his inquiry led him to. This is also the opinion of the Tashbetz (Ohev Mishpat 9) and the Ramak (Pardes Rimonim 1:9). And this is the opinion of most of the poskim in practice. Apparently, in their view only one who converted from his religion to idolatry has left the community of Israel with regard to the mitzvot of brotherhood. And even he is still called a son by God, and all the laws of Israel apply to him (see Responsa Rashba 1:194; 242).</p>\n<p>And perhaps even the Rambam would agree that in practice one should not exclude from the community of Israel one who does not believe in one of the principles, for it is almost always possible to judge him favourably, that perhaps he did not study and inquire enough, or that he did not have a rabbi to teach him properly. If so, even according to him only one who understood the principles well and decided to deny one of them has excluded himself from the community.<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"The words of the Rambam that by choosing to deny one of the thirteen principles a person leaves the community of Israel are cited in his Commentary on the Mishna in Sanhedrin, in the introduction to the chapter 'Ḥelek', and also in Hilkhot Rotze'aḥ 13:14; Hilkhot Avoda Zara 2:5; Hilkhot Mamrim 3:2. This is based on the baraita in Rosh Ha-shana 17a, that the heretics, the apikorsim, and those who deny the Torah and the resurrection of the dead are judged for all generations in Gehinnom and have no share in the World to Come. According to the Rambam, those who deny the thirteen principles of faith are included among the heretics, the apikorsim and those who deny the Torah. Many understood the Rambam's opinion to be that even one who clarified his outlook honestly and reached a conclusion that contradicts one of the principles is judged as a heretic who has left the community of Israel, and one should hate him (Abarbanel, Rosh Amana 12; Mirkevet Ha-mishneh (of Chelm), Hilkhot Teshuva 7:3; and so it emerges from Moreh Nevukhim 1:36). Concerning this understanding of the Rambam, our master Rav Kook wrote that the opinion of most of the poskim is not like the Rambam, and regarding the Abarbanel he wrote that he is \"an individual against the many and great among the poskim\" (Perek Be-hilkhot Tzibbur, Ma'amarei Ha-re'iya, pp. 55-61). Our master Rav Kook further wrote (Shemona Kevatzim 1:30-32) that \"as long as that corporealist does not make himself a graven image and a likeness, he has not completed his thought, and it remains still in the realm of the spirit, so that it cannot be included under the name of uprooting and leaving the religion.\" See further the words of our master Rav Kook in note 4.<br>However, there are also opinions about the Rambam's own view. Rabbi David Shiriro (of Salonica, died 5508) in 'Mishneh Kesef' (Hilkhot Teshuva 3:7) wrote that one could say that even according to the Rambam, one who errs in this is not considered a heretic: \"One may say that our master called a 'min' only one who intends to rebel against the Holy One, blessed be He, and to attribute to Him that He has a body and a form. And indeed, had he erred because of the imagery of the verses or of the aggadot, our master would concede that this is not a min.\" According to this, the words of the Rambam are directed at one who chose to deny the principles of the faith of Israel, such as Paul, the founder of Christian theology. Similarly Rabbi Me'iri Ha-me'ili [?] explained (Ma'amar Meshiv Nefesh 15) that only \"one who decides by the power of his intellect and by the determination of his mind that so it is in truth, and comes to dispute the sages of the Torah who understand and who received it, master from master, back to Moshe our teacher, peace be upon him\" is considered a heretic.<br>In addition, it is clear that even according to the Rambam (Hilkhot Teshuva 3:14), all those who deny the principles of faith can repent, and then they will have a share in the World to Come like all of Israel. One can also infer from the words of the Rambam (Hilkhot Mamrim 3:2) that relating to the heretic as one towards whom one need not act with brotherhood is not an intrinsic mitzva, but an act of defence against the danger that he poses to Judaism. For he wrote: \"For all these (the heretics and apikorsim) are not included in Israel, and there is need neither for witnesses, nor warning, nor judges; rather, whoever kills one of them has performed a great mitzva and removed the stumbling block.\" That is, defining them as those who \"are not of the community of Israel\", to the point of killing them, was intended to remove a stumbling block that endangers the community of Israel. But when distancing does not remove a stumbling block, and on the contrary, generally increases it, as actually happens in recent generations, the basic law returns: that they are Jews and are called children of God, and all the mitzvot of brotherhood apply to them."}]},{"slug":"20-30-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-09/","title_he":"ט – דעת מרן הרב קוק על הכופרים בעת החדשה","title_en":"The View of Our Master Rav Kook on the Heretics of the Modern Era","body_en":"<p>According to our master Rav Kook, one should not exclude from the community of Israel those who deny the principles of faith in recent generations, and this on the basis of two central foundations.</p>\n<p>The first: most heretics in the modern era do not leave by choice, but are rather like those acting under duress, since the spirit of the generation makes faith very difficult (Iggerot Ha-re'iya vol. 1, 138, pp. 170-171). This foundation is agreed upon by the great majority of rabbis, and many of them based it on an extension of the concept of \"a child who was taken captive among the gentiles\" (Shabbat 68b), who does not know the foundations of faith and halakha, and therefore regarding the transgressions he commits his status is that of one acting under duress. This is similar to what the Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Mamrim 3:2-3) concerning the children of the Karaites, that one should not distance them, even though the Karaites deny the eighth principle.<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>The second foundation is deeper: many times heresy stems from a mistaken and diminishing explanation of faith, and then their heresy is because they believe that there is a greater scientific and moral truth, and therefore they cannot accept a faith in God that does not rise to the truth and goodness to which they aspire. As our master Rav Kook wrote: \"There are many apikorsim who are heretics according to the measure of (the definitions of) halakha, but when we understand the depths of their soul, we find in them a connection to the divine content in a hidden form. And for this reason the inclination towards merit and kindness is very great in our generations, even towards those who are complete heretics\" (Shemona Kevatzim 1:327).</p>\n<p>Moreover, Rav Kook wrote: \"There is heresy that is like affirmation, and affirmation that is like heresy. How so? A person affirms that the Torah is from Heaven, but that Heaven is pictured by him in such strange forms that nothing whatsoever remains in it of true faith. And how is there heresy that is like affirmation? A person denies Torah from Heaven, but his denial is based only on the impression he absorbed from the picture of the form of Heaven in minds full of vain and empty thoughts.\" But in truth he believes in the values of truth and goodness, and believes that there is a correct way of life that is, from his point of view, 'the Torah'. Therefore \"he says: the Torah has a source more exalted than this (than what the representatives of religion present), and he begins to find its foundation in the greatness of the human spirit, in the depth of his ethics and the height of his wisdom.\" It is true that in practice \"he has not yet reached the centre of truth with this\", but \"in any case this heresy is like affirmation (of faith), it is important, and it is gradually drawing closer to the affirmation of steadfast faith.\" Therefore, together with the justified criticism of the heretics, this 'perverse generation' must also be interpreted favourably, in that their heresy is because of their belief in truth and goodness, which they perceive as higher (Shemona Kevatzim 1:633). Accordingly, our master Rav Kook concluded in practice that Jews holding heretical opinions should not be considered as having left the religion of Israel; rather, only one whose heresy led him to idol worship or to conversion to another religion is considered as one who has excluded himself from the community of Israel.<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"The poskim wrote that one should be lenient regarding the status of public Sabbath desecrators in recent generations, since the wall of Shabbat has been breached and desecrating Shabbat is not considered by them heresy and abandonment of Judaism as in the past (Melamed Le-ho'il 1:29; see Peninei Halakha Harḥavot, Shabbat 1:15:4). And in Responsa Binyan Tziyon (Ha-ḥadashot 23) it is written that one who was educated to desecrate Shabbat is considered a child taken captive among the gentiles, because the deeds of his fathers are in his hands, and therefore his touching wine does not render it forbidden. Many wrote likewise, as explained in Peninei Halakha, Kashrut 29:13, note 15. The Ḥazon Ish inclined to this (see YD 2:16; 28). There are those who disagree (see Peninei Halakha, Kashrut, ibid.), but in practice the attitude of the great majority of rabbis in our day is that transgressors who did not grow up on the knees of the Torah are considered as acting under duress, as we learned regarding including a secular Jew in a minyan (Peninei Halakha, Tefilla 2:8, note 10) or in a zimun (Peninei Halakha, Berakhot 5:8, note 13), and likewise in the laws of mourning (Rav Eliyahu's notes on the Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh 201:3), and in many more laws."},{"n":4,"en":"Our master Rav Kook wrote (Shemona Kevatzim 1:30-31): \"Although error in divine matters is a very harmful thing, nevertheless the principal harm that ensues from the distorted concepts does not come into actuality to the point of killing its holder with the death of the soul, except when it becomes embodied in deeds, or at least when it descends into such opinions and feelings as must in the end be revealed in deeds. But as long as the matter stands in its abstract form, there is no essential uprooting here. And in this we are close to the reasoning of the Ra'avad, who objected to the Rambam for calling one who believes in the corporeality of God a 'min'; and we can agree that as long as the corporealist does not make himself a graven image and a likeness, he has not completed his thought, and it remains still in the realm of the spirit, so that it cannot be included under the name of uprooting and leaving the religion.\" Rav Kook wrote concerning his words cited above (Shemona Kevatzim 1:633), in his explanation that there are those who deny Torah from Heaven but in truth they are believers, that this explanation is a paradigm and an example \"for all the general principles and details of the beliefs\", that sometimes the expression is one of heresy but the essence of the inner position is one of faith.<br>Our master Rav Kook further wrote that only one who betrays the community of Israel is considered as one who has separated from Israel, but to exclude transgressors and heretics from the community of Israel, and to claim \"that the nation includes only the good and the righteous alone – this is the way of heresy\" (Perek Be-hilkhot Tzibbur, Ma'amarei Ha-re'iya, pp. 55-61, see there). And in Iggeret 555 he wrote that the one who leaves the community of the chosen treasure of Israel is only one who has reached, God forbid, the point of being \"a hater of Israel, who seeks evil for them in deed and in the longing of his heart, like the heretics whom the Rambam explains in Hilkhot Tefilla, who oppressed Israel.\""}]},{"slug":"20-30-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-10/","title_he":"י – צמיחת האמונה מתוך הכפירה","title_en":"The Growth of Faith out of Heresy","body_en":"<p>Out of heresy, faith will grow and be renewed at a higher and purer level, for the heresy that appears to attack faith in truth attacks the husks and dross that darkened its light, to the point that people attributed to God the qualities of an honour-seeking dictator, who takes vengeance and bears a grudge against anyone who does not obey one of His instructions, and demands of man that he submit to Him and nullify all his talents and aspirations, even when they seem good to him. Against such a benighted faith, people who aspire to a life of freedom and creativity feel an obligation to rebel. In the process they also kick away the kernel of truth and goodness in faith and cause many troubles for themselves and for the world.</p>\n<p>Throughout the exile, it was not evident how great was the damage of the husks and dross that darkened faith. But when the time came for Israel to return to the Land of Israel, and for the Shekhina to return to dwell in the Land, the husks and dross became a great stumbling block, and against them a great heresy awoke.</p>\n<p>We must delve deeply into the study of faith and Torah, and refine faith of the dross and husks that have clung to it, and through this it will shine its light, and we will understand that God does not wish to crush man but to strengthen him. Therefore He created him in the image of God, so that he could be His partner in adding goodness and blessing to the world. Through this all who seek truth and goodness will be able to know that it is precisely through faith in God that they will be able to realise their aspirations in the most exalted way. Thus all of Israel will return to accept the Torah willingly and with love, out of clear knowledge that the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to grant them merit, and therefore gave them much Torah and many mitzvot, so that through them they could refine and intensify all their powers without end.</p>\n<p>In the following sections we will explain the four foundations on which Israel bases and roots faith: a) by way of the revelation in which God revealed Himself to His people Israel at the Sinai event and in all the events surrounding it, as explained in the Torah. b) by way of the history of the people of Israel, which is the history of the revelation of the word of God in the world. c) by way of contemplating creation with all its wonders, and at its centre man, who was created in the image of God. d) by way of walking in the ways of God according to the guidance of the Torah.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-30-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-11/","title_he":"יא – האמונה דרך ההתגלות לעם ישראל","title_en":"Faith through the Revelation to the People of Israel","body_en":"<p><b>The first foundation</b> is based on the revelation in which God revealed Himself to His people Israel, as related in the Torah. This way clarifies and refines the three other ways, for all human beings are believers by nature, but the meaning of faith is clarified through the divine revelation with which God revealed Himself to His people. The essence of the revelation was at the Sinai event, at which God gave the Torah to His people Israel, but all the events surrounding the Sinai event also explain faith and its meaning, as related in the Five Books of the Torah.</p>\n<p>First God revealed Himself to the Patriarchs, Avraham, Yitzḥak and Ya'akov, and chose them and their descendants to inherit the Land and to bring His blessing to the world, as explained in the book of Bereshit. The continuation of the revelation was when God redeemed the children of Israel from Egypt, and brought them out to freedom with signs and wonders in order to bring them up to the Land of Israel, as explained in the first portions of the book of Shemot. On their way to the Land, God brought Israel to Mount Sinai, and in a miraculous event gave them the Torah, as explained in further portions of the book of Shemot and in the book of Devarim. From all these great revelations we learned that God rules over all, and that He chose Israel to be His treasured people and gave them the Torah in a revelation that leaves no room for doubt (above 1:4-9). Afterwards, throughout their stay in the wilderness before their entry into the Land, God watched over Israel, protected them and sustained them, and caused His Shekhina to dwell upon them through the Mishkan that they erected at His command. Likewise, throughout the Torah are explained the mitzvot that God gave Israel at the Sinai event.</p>\n<p>Many mitzvot are intended to root in us the foundations of faith that are based on the divine revelation to the people of Israel. In order to remember the Exodus from Egypt, through which God revealed His power in the world and His choice of the people of Israel, we were commanded to celebrate the festival of Pesaḥ every year, and on the first night of Pesaḥ, which falls on the date on which we left Egypt, we were commanded to hold the Seder night in order to tell of the Exodus from Egypt. Likewise, we were commanded to remember the Exodus from Egypt in all the Kiddushim and prayers of the Sabbaths and festivals. In addition, we were commanded to remember the Exodus from Egypt every day and every night, and to put on tefillin, in whose passages the Exodus from Egypt is mentioned.</p>\n<p>In order to remember the Sinai event, at which God revealed Himself to Israel until they believed in Him with a faith that leaves no room for doubt, and through which He gave Israel His Torah – we were commanded to celebrate the festival of Shavuot on the day on which the event took place. Likewise we were commanded to recite Keri'at Shema every day, morning and night, in order to remember the foundations of faith that were revealed to us at the Sinai event. And we were commanded to put tefillin on our arm and head, and to affix mezuzot on the doorways of our homes, in which the foundations of the faith in God's unity appear. Also through the mitzva of Torah study, we continue to remember the Sinai event and the giving of the Torah.</p>\n<p>In order to remember God's providence over us throughout our wanderings in the wilderness, we were commanded to celebrate the festival of Sukkot. Through this we remember the Land that God gave as an inheritance to our fathers and to us, and in which God spreads His tabernacle of peace over us.</p>\n<p>In order to express the covenant that God made with Avraham and his descendants to reveal His name in the world, we were commanded to circumcise every male on the eighth day after his birth.</p>\n<p>In addition, one of the main aims of the mitzva to rest from melakha on the Sabbaths and festivals is that we be able to free ourselves for the study of Torah. As the Sages said (Yerushalmi Shabbat 15:3): \"Sabbaths and festivals were given only in order to engage in words of Torah on them.\" The intention is that we dedicate half the day to God in Torah study in the beit midrash, and half of it to the delight of Shabbat, with eating, drinking and sleeping (Pesaḥim 68b; Peninei Halakha, Shabbat 5:1-2; Mo'adim 1:5-6). And this is so that every Jew continue always to study the foundations of faith explained in the Torah, and all the mitzvot that God gave Israel, through which Israel can realise their destiny of adding goodness and blessing to the world.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-30-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-12/","title_he":"יב – האמונה דרך תולדות ישראל","title_en":"Faith through the History of Israel","body_en":"<p><b>The second foundation</b>: in addition to the great revelations with which God revealed Himself to His people Israel when they left Egypt and at the Sinai event, from then on the entire history of Israel is the history of the revelation of faith in the world. For this is the meaning of our being God's people, as it is said (Yeshayahu 43:21): \"This people I formed for Myself – they shall tell My praise\", and it is said (ibid. 43:10): \"You are My witnesses, says the Lord.\" And so it is said in the song of Ha'azinu (Devarim 32:7-9): \"Remember the days of old, consider the years of generation after generation. Ask your father and he will tell you, your elders and they will say to you. When the Most High gave the nations their inheritance, when He separated the children of man – He set the borders of the peoples according to the number of the children of Israel. For the Lord's portion is His people, Ya'akov the lot of His inheritance.\" That is, when Israel merit to reveal the word of God in the world through settling the Land and keeping the mitzvot, blessing is drawn through them to the world. And when Israel betray their role and sin, they are punished in their land, and if they do not repent they are punished with the gravest punishment – exile. As it is said (Devarim 11:13-14; 16-17): \"And it shall be, if you will diligently listen to My commandments which I command you this day… that I will give the rain of your land in its season, the early rain and the late rain, and you shall gather in your grain, your wine and your oil… Take heed to yourselves, lest your heart be deceived, and you turn aside and serve other gods and bow down to them. And the anger of the Lord be kindled against you, and He shut up the heavens so that there be no rain, and the ground not yield its produce, and you perish quickly from off the good land which the Lord gives you.\" (And so too in Bamidbar 33:53; 55-56, the punishment for neglecting the mitzva of settling the Land is exile.)</p>\n<p>Therefore the main activity of the prophets was to rouse Israel to keep the way of God, so that they would not be punished and exiled from their land. As it is said (II Melakhim 17:13): \"And the Lord warned Israel and Judah by the hand of every prophet and every seer, saying: Turn back from your evil ways and keep My commandments and My statutes, according to all the Torah which I commanded your fathers and which I sent to you by the hand of My servants the prophets.\" (And so in Yirmiyahu 7:23-25; 26:5; 29:19; 35:15; 44:4; II Divrei Ha-yamim 36:15-16, and elsewhere.)</p>\n<p>But God promised that even if Israel sin greatly, He will not break His covenant with them, and after they repent or after their punishment is completed – He will redeem them. As it is said (Vayikra 26:42): \"Then I will remember My covenant with Ya'akov, and also My covenant with Yitzḥak, and also My covenant with Avraham will I remember, and I will remember the land…\" And so it is said (Devarim 30:3-5): \"Then the Lord your God will return your captivity and have compassion upon you, and will return and gather you from all the peoples where the Lord your God has scattered you. If your dispersed be at the end of the heavens – from there the Lord your God will gather you and from there He will take you. And the Lord your God will bring you into the land which your fathers possessed, and you shall possess it, and He will do you good and multiply you more than your fathers.\"</p>\n<p>It is further explained in the prophet (Yeḥezkel 36:19-21; 23-24) that since in exile the name of God that is called upon Israel is desecrated, it is impossible that the exile continue forever, and therefore even if Israel do not repent properly, God will sanctify His name and through harsh suffering will return Israel to Him and redeem them. As it is said (Yeḥezkel 20:33-40): \"As I live, says the Lord God, surely with a mighty hand and with an outstretched arm and with fury poured out will I be king over you. And I will bring you out from the peoples and gather you from the countries… with a mighty hand and with an outstretched arm and with fury poured out… and I will bring you into the bond of the covenant. And I will purge from among you the rebels and those who transgress against Me… For on My holy mountain, on the mountain of the height of Israel, says the Lord God, there shall all the house of Israel, all of them, serve Me in the land…\"</p>\n<p>In order that we understand this matter in all its breadth and depth, we were commanded to study the divine revelations that were revealed to the world through the history of the people of Israel. They begin in the Tanakh and continue in the words of the Sages up to our day. In order to root this in our consciousness, the prophets instituted the fasts of the destruction: the Tenth of Tevet, the Seventeenth of Tammuz, Tisha Be-Av and the Fast of Gedalya. Likewise the Sages instituted the days of Purim in order to thank God for saving us from the hands of the kingdom of Persia, and the days of Ḥanukka in order to thank God for our deliverance from the kingdom of Greece.</p>\n<p>Out of understanding this deep foundation, which deals with the bond between God and Israel His people, one can learn and establish the bond between God and man and all of humanity. Indeed, it is a wondrous fact that the history of Israel is the basis for the revelation of faith in the two great religions – Christianity and Islam. But since, because of our many sins, we were exiled from our land, their believers thought that they were meant to replace us. But by the kindness of God, in recent generations the divine promise of redemption has begun to be fulfilled, and from the four corners of the earth the people of Israel is returning to its land, to build it and make its desolate places bloom, and the word of God and His blessing are being increasingly revealed through His people Israel. Speedily may we merit the fulfilment of the words of the prophecy (Yeshayahu 2:3): \"And many peoples shall go and say: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord, to the house of the God of Ya'akov, and He will teach us of His ways and we will walk in His paths, for out of Zion shall go forth Torah and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem.\"</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-30-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-13/","title_he":"יג – האמונה דרך ההתבוננות בבריאה","title_en":"Faith through Contemplating Creation","body_en":"<p><b>The third foundation</b> for establishing faith is remembering the creation of the world and contemplating the wondrous divine wisdom that is revealed in all of creation: in the expanses of space and the galaxies, in the tiny cell and the particles of atoms, in seas and continents, in plants and animals; in the wonder, the sublimity, the might and the beauty of creation. \"The heavens declare the glory of God, and the firmament tells the work of His hands\" (Tehillim 19:2). Through this a person is filled with faith and gratitude to God. As David said: \"Bless the Lord, O my soul; Lord my God, You are very great… How manifold are Your works, O Lord; in wisdom have You made them all; the earth is full of Your possessions. This is the sea, great and wide, there are creeping things without number, living creatures small and great…\" (Tehillim 104:1; 24-25). This contemplation also intensifies in a person the love of God and the fear of Him, as the Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 2:2): \"And what is the way to love Him and fear Him? When a person contemplates His great and wondrous works and creatures, and sees from them His wisdom, which has no measure and no end – immediately he loves, praises, glorifies and longs with a great longing to know the great Name, as David said (Tehillim 42:3): My soul thirsts for God, for the living God. And when he reflects on these very things, immediately he recoils, and is afraid and fearful, and knows that he is a small, lowly, dark creature, standing with slight and meagre knowledge before the One perfect in knowledge, as David said (Tehillim 8:4-5): When I see Your heavens, the work of Your fingers… what is man that You are mindful of him…\"</p>\n<p>Included in contemplating the work of God is also the study of the natural sciences, which is called 'Ma'aseh Bereshit' (Rambam, Commentary on the Mishna, Ḥagiga 2:1; Maharal, Netiv Ha-Torah 14). As the Sages said (Shabbat 75a), that anyone who is able to study the wisdoms of nature, such as the wisdom of astronomy, and does not study them, \"concerning him Scripture says (Yeshayahu 5:12): They do not regard the work of the Lord, nor have they seen the work of His hands.\" And so our master Rav Kook wrote that since the sciences bring \"to the knowledge of God and to the love of God, if so they are included in the command of 'Shema' and 'You shall love', concerning which is said 'And these words which I command you this day shall be upon your heart'\" (Shemona Kevatzim 1:900).</p>\n<p>In order to establish this foundation in our lives, the Sages determined that as part of the prayer we recite psalms of thanksgiving and praise to God for the wonder of creation and for all the good in life, such as 'Pesukei De-zimra' and the first blessing of the blessings of Keri'at Shema. Likewise the Sages instituted that every person bless God for all the good things from which he derives pleasure, from the enjoyment of food to the enjoyment of fragrance. And likewise he should recite blessings of praise to God when he sees the wonders of creation, such as seas, rivers and special landscapes.</p>\n<p>One must further contemplate man, the crown of creation, for faith is revealed even through the form of his body, as it is said (Iyov 19:26): \"And from my flesh I shall behold God.\" All the more so faith is revealed through contemplating man in all the variety of his talents and his wondrous achievements throughout history, which include: science, research, literature and art. And above all, ethics and the idealistic aspirations, which express the image of God in man.</p>\n<p>The mitzva of Shabbat also roots this foundation in our lives, for on Shabbat we remember that God created the world in six days and rested on the seventh day, and out of delight and rest we study in the Torah about the destiny of man, who was created in the image of God.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-30-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-30-14/","title_he":"יד – האמונה המתגלה על ידי ההליכה בדרכי ה'","title_en":"The Faith Revealed through Walking in the Ways of God","body_en":"<p><b>The fourth foundation</b> for establishing faith extends over all the expanses of life: that in all his ways a person should walk in the ways of God, draw down His light and blessing, reveal the image of God within him, and be a partner with God in settling the world and developing it, and thereby he will cleave to God and faith will give him life. As it is said (Devarim 13:5): \"After the Lord your God shall you walk… and to Him shall you cleave.\" The Sages asked (Sota 14a): \"Is it possible for a person to walk after the Shekhina? Has it not already been said (Devarim 4:24): 'For the Lord your God is a consuming fire'! Rather, to walk after the attributes of the Holy One, blessed be He.\" Just as He clothes the naked – so you too clothe the naked; just as He visits the sick – so you too visit the sick; just as He comforts mourners – so you too comfort mourners, and the like.</p>\n<p>Likewise the Sages said (Vayikra Rabba 25:3) that the way to cleave to God is through engaging in the settlement of the world. Just as He, \"from the beginning of the creation of the world, occupied Himself first only with planting… so you too – when you enter the Land of Israel, occupy yourselves first only with planting\", as it is said (Vayikra 19:23): \"And when you come into the land and plant every tree for food.\" And so every person should intend in his work to add goodness and blessing to the world: if he is a builder – he should strive to build strong and good houses; if he is a farmer – he should grow healthy and tasty fruit; and if he makes furniture – he should make good and comfortable furniture. Likewise merchants should intend in their commerce to add goodness and blessing, and take care not to deceive in their dealings, and thereby they will cleave to the attribute of God, whose seal is truth (Shabbat 55a). And if he is a scientist, he should intend to reveal the depths of the Creator's wisdom in creation, and aspire that from these discoveries blessing be drawn for man and society. Also with the money a person earns through his work, he should add goodness and blessing: support his family and educate his children in Torah and proper conduct, set aside a tithe from it for the upholding and glorification of Torah, and support the poor with it. And if he grows fruit, he should set aside from it terumot and ma'asrot for the kohanim, the Levites and the poor.</p>\n<p>In his marriage too he should strive to be loving and faithful, for in this he walks in the ways of God, since the idea of love and unity is drawn from the love with which God loves His creatures, and from the one root of all creatures. As the Sages said, a man and a woman who are faithful to and love one another – the Shekhina dwells between them (Sota 17a). In bearing children too, in educating them and supporting them, the parents cleave to God and become partners with Him in adding life to the world. The Sages said (Kiddushin 30b): \"There are three partners in a person: the Holy One, blessed be He, his father and his mother. When a person honours his father and his mother, the Holy One, blessed be He, says: I consider it as though I dwelt among them and they honoured Me.\" In this we learned that the mitzva of honouring parents too is bound up with the honour of Heaven.</p>\n<p>There are further statutes, such as the prohibition against eating various foods, for example impure animals or meat with milk, through whose observance we cleave to God in our eating too. In order that we be able to cleave to God in all our ways, God gave us His Torah and His mitzvot, and through perseverance and depth in the study of Torah and diligence in the fulfilment of the mitzvot, we merit to establish faith in our lives, to cleave to God and to act for the repair of the world. This is what is said (Mishlei 3:6): \"In all your ways know Him, and He will make your paths straight.\" The Sages said (Berakhot 63a): \"This is a small passage on which all the essentials of the Torah depend.\"</p>\n<p>May it be His will that faith illuminate our lives, and that out of full identification and not out of fear we walk in the ways of God, engage in Torah and mitzvot, settle the Land, and do justice and righteousness. Out of this we will pray to God that He shine a new light upon Zion, and that we all merit to cleave to Him with pure will and without any coercion, as it is said (Hoshea 2:18-22): \"And it shall be on that day, says the Lord, that you shall call Me 'my husband', and shall no more call Me 'my master'. For I will remove the names of the Ba'alim from her mouth, and they shall no more be remembered by their name. And on that day I will make a covenant for them with the beasts of the field and with the birds of the heavens and with the creeping things of the ground, and I will break the bow and the sword and war out of the land, and will make them lie down safely. And I will betroth you to Me forever; and I will betroth you to Me in righteousness and in justice and in kindness and in compassion. And I will betroth you to Me in faithfulness, and you shall know the Lord.\" And God will remove all the partitions that arose in the wake of sin, and the divine light will shine and give life forever, as it is said (Yeshayahu 25:8-9): \"He will swallow up death forever, and the Lord God will wipe away tears from off all faces, and the reproach of His people He will remove from off all the earth, for the Lord has spoken. And (Israel) will say on that day: Behold, this is our God, we have waited for Him and He will save us; this is the Lord, we have waited for Him, we will rejoice and be glad in His salvation.\"</p>","notes_en":[]}]});
